The 6 Core Exam Domains
1. Manage Resource Lifecycle [1]
Focuses on operational control, handling drift, and resolving infrastructure delivery blocks. [1, 2]
Plan and Apply Mechanics: Generating, saving, and targeting explicit execution plans (terraform plan -out).
State Manipulation: Refactoring state safely via terraform state mv, terraform state rm, and handling targeted manual resource deletions.
Drift Resolution: Identifying discrepancies between actual infrastructure configurations and state files, and utilizing terraform import blocks to bring outside resources into HCL management. [1, 2]
2. Develop and Troubleshoot Dynamic Configurations [1]
Tests your ability to write clean, advanced, and dry (Don't Repeat Yourself) code structures. [1]
Dynamic Blocks: Writing complex, nested loops with dynamic blocks to generate repetitive sub-arguments dynamically.
Complex Collections: Manipulating data variables using built-in HCL functions (merge, flatten, try, can).
Troubleshooting: Enabling and interpreting diagnostic traces (TF_LOG=DEBUG) to isolate engine syntax errors, API rate limits, or provider credential failures. [1, 2, 3, 4]
3. Develop Collaborative Workflows [1]
Addresses the operational strategies required to manage infrastructure across multi-stage corporate structures. [1]
Monorepo vs. Multi-Repo: Managing enterprise repository layouts and structuring localized workflows.
HCP Terraform Workspaces: Configuring remote execution environments, handling environment run triggers, and passing outputs across independent states.
Concurrency: Handling state-locking mechanisms across teams to avoid concurrent write corruption. [1, 2, 3, 4]
4. Create and Maintain Terraform Modules [1, 2]
Validates advanced module development patterns for internal infrastructure catalogs. [1]
Module Composition: Building reusable, nested multi-tier module architectures.
Version Control: Enforcing strict version constraints, sourcing tags from git remotes, and managing breaking upgrades safely.
Testing and Validation: Implementing input variable custom validations using validation blocks and syntax conditions. [1, 2, 3, 4]
5. Configure Terraform Providers [1]
Focuses on how the engine speaks to target platforms, using the AWS Provider as the primary focus. [1]
Multi-Provider & Aliases: Configuring multiple instances of a provider to handle multi-region architectures or multi-account deployments via HCL aliases.
Custom Configurations: Handling strict default tag propagation, configuring provider timeouts, and handling mock credentials. [1, 2]
6. Collaborate on Infrastructure as Code Using HCP Terraform [1]
Focuses on cloud platform management tools, governance, and organizational scaling. [1, 2]
Policy as Code: Writing and enforcing Sentinel or Open Policy Agent (OPA) governance check rules to block non-compliant resource plans.
Run Tasks & Security: Integrating external automated third-party tools (security scanners, cost estimation tools) directly into the HCP pipeline



